==Solution 11==

We take our <imath>120</imath> cases and subtract the cases with <imath>3</imath> increasing or <imath>3</imath> decreasing. By symmetry, we can find the amount for one and multiply the number by <imath>2</imath>. 

Originally, we find that there are <imath>10</imath> choices for the numbers we will choose to be increasing, <imath>3</imath> choices for where to place them, and <imath>2</imath> choices for where to place the remaining numbers, for a total of <imath>60</imath> ways. However, we have overcounted all of the cases with multiple triplets of increasing integers in a row. We write these out and find that we have overcounted by <imath>10</imath> ways, for an actual total of <imath>50</imath> ways. Be careful at this step, as for certain cases like <imath>12345</imath> we actually overcounted by <imath>2</imath>.

We multiply this by <imath>2</imath> to find that there are also <imath>50</imath> ways for the decreasing.

However, we have now overcounted the cases in which there are increasing and decreasing triplets.

We break these cases into <imath>2</imath> different groups: the first group is where the first <imath>3</imath> integers are decreasing, and the last triplet will be increasing. This forces the middle number to be <imath>1</imath>; we quickly write out the cases and find <imath>6</imath>.

The second case is where the first <imath>3</imath> are increasing and the second <imath>3</imath> are decreasing. This means the middle number can be a <imath>5</imath>. We write out our cases for these and find another <imath>6</imath> cases, for a grand total of <imath>12</imath> overcounted cases. This means there are actually a total of <imath>50+50-12=88</imath> distinct cases where there are <imath>3</imath> increasing in a row or <imath>3</imath> decreasing.

We now subtract these <imath>88</imath> cases from <imath>120</imath> to get our answer of <imath>\boxed{32}</imath>.

~jamespudi

